covers the rest of the repository.


1. humanizer-de 5.28.1

The source is https://github.com/marmbiz/humanizer-de at tag v5.28.1, by
Martin Moeller. Portions of it derive from blader/humanizer by Siqi Chen.

humanizer-de ships its scripts and original material under MIT. It ships
references/patterns.md and the matching pattern tables under CC BY-SA 4.0.
Those tables adapt the Wikipedia project pages "Anzeichen für KI-generierte
Inhalte" (German) and "Signs of AI writing" (English). The humanizer-de
NOTICE file states this split.

The German rule trigger lists here follow the humanizer-de patterns closely.
They keep the same patterns and trigger words (decision Q13). We wrote the
rule descriptions in our own words. A code comment names the humanizer-de
pattern number (Muster N) next to each borrowed trigger list.

These files carry that material.

  hooks/lib/german_rules/chatbot.py
  hooks/lib/german_rules/format.py
  hooks/lib/german_rules/markup.py
  hooks/lib/german_rules/phrases.py
  hooks/lib/german_rules/rhetoric.py
  hooks/lib/german_rules/semantic_claims.py
  hooks/lib/german_rules/words.py
  docs/research/2026-09-30-german-style-catalog.md, rules 21 to 80


2. German style books, rules in our own words

The German rules below restate points from these books in our own words.
No sentence from them ships in this repository.

  Monika Salchert, Verständliches Schreiben. Selbstlernheft. Bundesakademie
  für öffentliche Verwaltung im Bundesministerium des Innern (BAköV),
  2nd edition, September 2012.

  Andreas Baumert, Professionell texten. dtv Ratgeber, C.H. Beck.

  Stefan Gottschling, Einfach besser texten. GABAL, 2006.

Rules 1 to 20 of docs/research/2026-09-30-german-style-catalog.md cite
these books, and the German rule modules implement those rows.


3. Duden

The source is Dudenredaktion, Einfach können. Rechtschreibung,
Zeichensetzung und Grammatik. Cornelsen, 2025. ISBN 978-3-411-75696-4.

hooks/lib/german_punctuation.py and hooks/lib/catalog_de.py restate German
typography rules in our own words. These cover the Gedankenstrich, the
Bis-Strich, the Ergänzungsstrich, German quotation marks, the ellipsis, and
the genitive apostrophe. No Duden text ships.


4. German exemplar sentences

hooks/lib/pattern_exemplars_de.jsonl stores one sentence per row. The
"source" field of each row names one of these four sources.

  dewiki-20180920 holds German Wikipedia sentences from the 2018-09-20 dump
  at https://archive.org/download/dewiki-20180920. Wikipedia licensed that
  text under CC BY-SA 3.0 and GFDL.

  sedthh/gutenberg_multilang holds German public-domain books from Project
  Gutenberg, read through
  https://huggingface.co/datasets/sedthh/gutenberg_multilang. Its dataset
  card states MIT for the collection.

  wildchat holds German assistant replies from
  https://huggingface.co/datasets/allenai/WildChat-4.8M under ODC-BY.

  coling holds German machine-written text from the COLING 2025 GenAI
  Content Detection Task 1 data, read through
  https://huggingface.co/datasets/Jinyan1/COLING_2025_MGT_multingual. The
  task repository at
  https://github.com/mbzuai-nlp/COLING-2025-Workshop-on-MGT-Detection-Task1
  carries Apache-2.0. The Hugging Face dataset card states no license.

hooks/lib/pattern_exemplars_de.json holds hashes, counts, and precision
numbers. It stores no third-party text.


5. English exemplar sentences

hooks/lib/pattern_exemplars.jsonl stores one sentence per row. The "origin"
field of each row names one of these sources.

  assistant/wildchat comes from
  https://huggingface.co/datasets/allenai/WildChat-4.8M under ODC-BY.

  assistant/arena comes from
  https://huggingface.co/datasets/lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-100k.
  Its dataset card puts user prompts under CC BY 4.0, and model outputs
  under the terms of use of each model provider.

  human/encyclopedia, human/literature, and human/news come from local
  files named Wikipedia.csv, Gutenberg.csv, and CNN_DailyMail.csv, read by
  evals/build_human_corpus.py. The repository does not record where these
  files came from. Wikipedia text carries CC BY-SA. Gutenberg text is in
  the public domain. CNN and the Daily Mail hold the copyright in their
  articles.


6. German Wiktionary

evals/syllable_counter.json records the syllable counter error rate against
the Worttrennung field of https://de.wiktionary.org. The Wiktionary Impressum
names CC BY-SA 3.0 and GFDL. The file stores six German words with their
reference syllable counts, and no definitions or other Wiktionary text.


7. Label files

These label files store corpus row references, labels, and short rater
reasons, not the corpus sentences. A reason can quote a few words of the
sentence it judges.

  evals/pattern_labels_de.jsonl
  evals/pattern_labels_de_sonnet.jsonl
  evals/pattern_labels_de_adjudicated.jsonl
  evals/german_static_sample.jsonl
  evals/german_static_labels_sonnet.jsonl
  evals/german_static_labels_luna.jsonl
  evals/german_static_labels_adjudicated.jsonl

Git ignores the corpora themselves, and they rebuild from their sources.
evals/README.md describes the rebuild.


